# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""The Duty of the Son of Heaven — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter Two — The Duty of the Son of Heaven'
HEAD = '''Fifteen passages, the longest chapter and the one with the most historical matter in it. The title says “the duty of the Son of Heaven,” but the subject is instruction and demeanour: no one may be used who has not first been taught; rank, virtue, skill and courage each keep within their bounds and do not encroach on one another, “and so strength is exerted as one and intentions agree.” The middle of the chapter is a run of four comparisons across the three dynasties — where Yu, Xia, Yin and Zhou swore their armies in, how far each went in drawing the blade, where each gave rewards and where it carried out executions; what each called its war chariot, what colour each flew, and whether its standard bore the sun and moon, a tiger or a dragon. These are pages no student of early Chinese military institutions can go round, and they are the reason the Han bibliography filed this book under rites. The hinge of the chapter is a sentence given twice: <i>the demeanour of the court does not enter the army, and the demeanour of the army does not enter the court</i> — and if they are exchanged, “the army’s demeanour in the state ruins the people’s virtue, the court’s demeanour in the army weakens it.” The last passages turn unexpectedly gentle: a great victory is not rewarded and a great defeat is not punished, because in either case both ranks take the blame on themselves — “deference at its height.”'''

PARA = {
 'p2-1': 'The duty of the Son of Heaven is to take his model, purely, from heaven and earth, and to look to the sages before him. The duty of knight and commoner is to serve their father and mother and to take their correction from lord and elder. Therefore, even under an enlightened ruler, a man who has not first been taught cannot be used. (“Purely” is “with nothing mixed in.” The graph used here for “model” is the archaic form of the word for law; this book uses the archaic and the ordinary form within a single sentence, and so do both old witnesses, so neither has been regularised — see the colophon. Heaven and earth above, father and mother below, lord and elder between; and then the conclusion the whole chapter grows from — untaught, he cannot be used.)',
 'p2-2': 'In antiquity, in teaching the people, they first established the orders of high and low so that these should not encroach on one another; virtue and rightness did not overstep one another; talent and skill did not eclipse one another; courage and strength did not trespass on one another. And so strength was exerted as one and intentions agreed. (The Siku text reads “and so their quarters were one and intentions agreed.” The three “did not” clauses are the agenda; the next passage answers them one by one.)',
 'p2-3': 'In antiquity the demeanour of the court did not enter the army and the demeanour of the army did not enter the court, and so virtue and rightness did not overstep one another. Those above prized the man who did not boast of his deeds; the man who does not boast is the instrument of those above him. If he does not boast he wants nothing; wanting nothing, he does not contend. Cases heard in the state get at the facts; cases heard in the army get at what the moment requires — and so talent and skill did not eclipse one another. To obey orders earned a knight the highest reward, to break them the severest punishment — and so courage and strength did not trespass on one another. (The three headings opened in the previous passage are answered here in order: virtue and rightness, talent and skill, courage and strength.)',
 'p2-4': 'Once the teaching had been given to the people, they were chosen with care and then employed. When affairs are ordered to the last degree, the hundred offices suffice; when instruction is pared to the last degree, the people rise to goodness; when habit has been formed, the people make the custom their own body. This is instruction at its height. (“Make the custom their own body” — the third clause is the deepest of the three, and the most literal rendering is the best one.)',
 'p2-5': 'In antiquity they did not pursue far and did not press a withdrawal closely. Not pursuing far, they were hard to decoy; not pressing closely, they were hard to trap. They made the rites their firmness and benevolence their victory; and when they had won, their teaching could still be applied afterwards — which is why the gentleman valued it. (The Siku text has, for “a withdrawal,” the same word used in chapter one for “a withdrawing enemy.” This passage supplies a very practical reason for the hundred paces of chapter one: not only to observe the rites, but to avoid the ambush.)',
 'p2-6': 'The house of Yu gave its warning inside the state, wishing the people to take the command into themselves. The house of Xia swore its oath within the camp, wishing the people to settle their minds beforehand. The Yin swore outside the gate of the camp, wishing the people to fix their intention before acting. The Zhou swore when the blades were about to cross, to drive the people’s will to its pitch. The house of Xia rectified its own virtue and did not yet use the edge of the weapon, and so its weapons were unmixed. The Yin stood on rightness, and began to use the edge. The Zhou stood on strength, and used the edge to the full. Xia gave rewards at court, prizing the good; Yin carried out executions in the market, to overawe the bad; Zhou rewarded at court and executed in the market, to encourage the gentleman and put fear into the small man. In making their virtue manifest the three kings were doing one and the same thing. (The oaths are those of the <i>Book of Documents</i> — the oaths at Gan, at Tang, at Muye. The Siku text reads “wishing the people to fix their intention and <i>await</i> the event.” The place of the oath moves steadily inwards, from the capital to the camp gate to the moment of contact: the later the age, the nearer the blade one must go to raise the spirit.)',
 'p2-7': 'If weapons are not mixed, they are not effective. Long weapons are for covering, short ones for holding. Too long, and a weapon is hard to wield; too short, and it does not reach. Too light, and it is keen — and keenness leads to disorder; too heavy, and it is blunt — and bluntness does not do the work. (This turns the preceding passage over: there “unmixed weapons” were a virtue of the house of Xia; here they are a defect of equipment. It is the only passage in the book on weapons as such.)',
 'p2-8': 'As for war chariots: the house of Xia called its chariot the Hook Chariot, putting correctness first; the Yin called theirs the Yin Chariot, putting speed first; the Zhou called theirs the Great Chariot, putting excellence first. (The Great Chariot appears in the <i>Book of Songs</i>: “Ten great chariots go first to open the way.” Each dynasty names its chariot for one quality — correct, swift, good.)',
 'p2-9': 'As for the bannered flag: the house of Xia had a black head to it, which is what man holds to; the Yin had white, which is the rightness of heaven; the Zhou had yellow, which is the way of earth. (The flag here is the kind hung with bells. The Siku text has, for “what man holds to,” a graph meaning power or circumstance. That Xia honoured black, Yin white and Zhou red is the usual account; yellow for Zhou here goes with the doctrine that Zhou reigned by the virtue of earth.)',
 'p2-10': 'As for the standard: the house of Xia bore the sun and moon on it, honouring brightness; the Yin bore a tiger, which is “white and martial”; the Zhou bore a dragon, honouring pattern. (The base text and the Sibu congkan facsimile both give “white and martial” for the middle clause, which is hard to construe; the Siku text reads “honouring <i>awe</i>,” exactly matching “honouring brightness” and “honouring pattern” on either side. This page emends only where both witnesses agree, so the base reading stands. If “white and martial” is to be defended, it must be by white as the colour of the west and the tiger as the beast of the west, war being the western business. Both readings are left with the reader.)',
 'p2-11': 'If an army makes too much of severity the people are cowed; if it makes too little of it they are of no use. When those above employ the people beyond what is right for them, when the common people do not get their proper order, when skills find no use, when the oxen and horses are asked for more than they can give, and the officers ride over them into the bargain — this is called too much severity, and too much severity cows the people. When those above do not honour virtue but employ deceit, do not honour the Way but employ brute force, do not value obedience but value disobedience, do not value good conduct but value violence — and are themselves ridden over by their officers — this is called too little severity, and too little severity makes the people useless. In the army unhurriedness is the ruling thing. Unhurried, the people’s strength is sufficient; and then, even when the blades have crossed, the foot do not run, the chariots do not gallop, the pursuit of the routed does not break the ranks — and so there is no disorder. The firmness of an army lies in not losing the discipline of its ranks, in not using up the strength of men and horses, and in letting neither slowness nor speed exceed the command. (“Unhurried” is the single most important word in the book’s account of handling troops. The Sibu congkan text has a different graph in “exceed the command.” Two opposite diseases, too much severity and too little, and one medicine for both.)',
 'p2-12': 'In antiquity the demeanour of the court did not enter the army and the demeanour of the army did not enter the court. The army’s demeanour in the state ruins the people’s virtue; the court’s demeanour in the army weakens it. Therefore in the state one speaks with refinement and in a mild tone; at court one is respectful and yielding; one cultivates oneself in order to wait upon others; one does not come unsummoned and does not speak unasked; one is slow to advance and quick to withdraw. In the army one stands upright and unbending; in the ranks one is decisive; the armoured man does not bow; on a war chariot one does not make the salute at the rail; on the walls one does not go at the ceremonial trot; in danger one does not defer by age. Therefore rites and law are the outside and the inside of one thing, and civil and military are its left hand and its right. (The Siku text has a different graph in “in the ranks one is decisive.” The last two clauses are the verdict of the chapter: not two things, but two faces of one.)',
 'p2-13': 'In antiquity the worthy kings made the people’s virtue plain and drew out the whole of their goodness, so that no virtue was left waste and no one of the people was slighted; rewards had no occasion to arise, and punishments no occasion to be tried. The house of Yu neither rewarded nor punished, and its people could be used: virtue at its height. Xia rewarded but did not punish: instruction at its height. Yin punished but did not reward: awe at its height. Zhou used both reward and punishment: virtue in decline. Rewards were not held over beyond their season, so that the people should quickly learn the profit of doing good; punishments were not carried out away from the ranks, so that the people should quickly see the harm of doing ill. After a great victory there was no reward, and neither rank boasted of the merit: those above, not boasting, were not made proud; those below, not boasting, did not press their claims. When neither rank boasts to this degree, that is deference at its height. After a great defeat there was no execution, and both ranks took the fault upon themselves: those above, taking the fault on themselves, were sure to repent of it; those below, taking the fault on themselves, were sure to keep clear of it. When both ranks share the blame to this degree, that too is deference at its height. (“Not carried out away from the ranks” means punishment on the spot, without dragging the man out of the line. The Sibu congkan scribe writes a near-identical graph for “oneself” three times over. “A great victory is not rewarded, a great defeat is not punished” is at once the least military and the most military passage in the book: it is not managing this battle but the next one.)',
 'p2-14': 'In antiquity the garrison troops were not called up again for three years, because the hardship of the people had been seen. When those above and those below answer one another like this, that is concord at its height. (The Sibu congkan text reads “garrison <i>army</i>”; the Siku text has a different graph for “called up.”)',
 'p2-15': 'When the aim has been attained, the victory song is sung, to show gladness; the war is laid down and the Numinous Terrace raised, in answer to the people’s labour, to show rest. (The Numinous Terrace is King Wen’s, in the <i>Book of Songs</i>, a place where he took his pleasure with the people. The chapter ends on two actions only: a song and a building.)',
}
